+### 6. Children and the narrow one-shot amendment
+
+The original blanket headless prohibition and board-owned exec machinery are superseded only for the versioned Swarma agent-account one-shot mode accepted by D-EXECUTOR on 2026-10-11. Caller-side composition and interactive hosting retain their existing roles.
+
+#### Agent-account one-shot amendment (D-EXECUTOR, 2026-10-11)
+
+The caller composes and resolves the profile into an immutable plan. Under the agent account `curator-run` verifies and executes that plan only: no profile composition or resolution, and no network except the broker. A dedicated entry such as `curator-run exec --plan-fd N` illustrates the boundary; it is not published command syntax. The launcher owner must freeze a separate versioned intake/release, descriptor transport, verification/refusals and receipts before activation. The helper's specified FD3 plan delivery does not by itself publish the executor entry.
+
+The first broker consumer is agent-account `curator-run`. Board spawn and session host ask `swarma-dispatcher`, which admits/provisions/tracks and launches through the approved executor using `swarma-user-launcher`. They do not embed the first broker client. One launch library is shared with `swarma-session-runner`; a separate minimal executor binary remains a later option.
 
-Tracked non-interactive children stay with `task-board spawn` and its own
-exec machinery. There is no headless `curator run` (Decision 0019).
